@* The command palette: Ctrl-K from anywhere, type, press Enter.

   It offers the six creation steps, and — once something has been typed — the Powers, and for
   a reader who is signed in the rulebook's own passages. What it offers and in what order is
   decided in the service behind it, not here; this file is the surface.

   **It is rendered once, by the layout, rather than per page.** A palette that existed only
   on some steps would be an affordance a reader could not rely on, and reliability is the
   whole value of a keyboard shortcut.

   **The book grew this file and the service, and not `js/palette.js`.** That script says in as
   many words to resist growing it, and it has not grown by a byte — it is still one listener,
   two focus calls and one question about the keyboard, and there is a test holding it to
   exactly that. What a corpus needs is a request, a pause, a race guard and three more rows,
   and every one of those is a decision about what the palette offers, which has always been
   decided in `Commands` and drawn here. *@

@implements IAsyncDisposable
@inject Commands Commands
@inject NavigationManager Nav
@inject Shortcuts Keys

@if (Commands.IsOpen)
{
    @* The scrim is the click-outside target. It carries no role and no label: it is not a
       control, and announcing it would put a nameless element between the reader and the
       box. Escape is the keyboard's way out, handled on the box below. *@
    <div class="palette-scrim" @onclick="Close">
        @* stopPropagation, or a click on the box itself closes the palette under the reader's
           own cursor — which reads as the app falling over rather than as a mis-click. *@
        <div class="palette" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-label="Go to"
             @onclick:stopPropagation="true">

            @* The combobox pattern rather than roving focus: the caret stays in the box the
               whole time, so typing never stops working, and which row is current is carried
               by aria-activedescendant instead of by where focus is. That is what lets the
               arrow keys and the letters share one control. *@
            <input type="search"
                   class="palette-box"
                   role="combobox"
                   aria-expanded="true"
                   aria-controls="palette-results"
                   aria-activedescendant="@ActiveId"
                   aria-autocomplete="list"
                   aria-label="@Prompt"
                   placeholder="@Prompt"
                   value="@_query"
                   @oninput="Typed"
                   @onkeydown="Key"
                   @ref="_box" />

            <ul class="palette-results" id="palette-results" role="listbox"
                aria-label="Matches">
                @for (var i = 0; i < _found.Count; i++)
                {
                    var index = i;
                    var command = _found[i];

                    @* The book's rows are marked off from the app's own, because they are two
                       kinds of thing: above this line are places to go and controls to open,
                       below it are the publisher's words. Nothing on the page should leave the
                       two looking like one voice — the same reason the entry beside a Power is
                       set apart as a quotation rather than run in with the mechanics.

                       `role="presentation"` and no id: it is decoration, and the listbox's
                       children stay options alone so `aria-activedescendant` keeps naming a row
                       and the indices below stay the indices of `_found`. What it says is not
                       load-bearing for a screen reader, because every row it heads carries its
                       own chapter and page in its detail line. *@
                    if (GroupHeading(index) is { } heading)
                    {
                        <li class="palette-group" role="presentation">@heading</li>
                    }

                    <li id="@RowId(index)"
                        role="option"
                        class="@(index == _active ? "palette-row current" : "palette-row")"
                        aria-selected="@(index == _active ? "true" : "false")"
                        @onclick="() => Run(command)">
                        <span class="palette-label">@command.Label</span>
                        @if (command.Detail is not null)
                        {
                            <span class="palette-detail">@command.Detail</span>
                        }
                    </li>
                }
            </ul>

            @* **Not while the book is still being asked.** The sentence is about everything the
               palette offers, and for a signed-in reader one of those things is a question that
               has not come back yet — so printing it early says "nothing matches" and then
               replaces it with five rows, which reads as the app changing its mind rather than as
               an answer arriving. Nothing is drawn in its place: a spinner for a fifth of a second
               is worse than a box that has simply not answered yet.

               **Nor when the book could not be asked at all.** The sentence is a claim about the
               corpus, and a session that expired server-side, a 500 or a laptop off the network
               are not the corpus saying anything — printing it there tells a reader the rulebook
               has no entry for a word it may well have three of. That state is quiet on purpose:
               no rows and no sentence, because "the book could not be reached" over an open
               palette is an error report for something the reader did not ask for. *@
            @if (_found.Count == 0 && !Commands.BookIsBeingAsked && !Commands.BookCouldNotBeAsked)
            {
                <p class="palette-empty">Nothing here matches what you typed.</p>
            }

            @* The keys, written down where somebody meeting the palette will see them. A
               shortcut nobody is told about is a shortcut for whoever wrote it.

               Spans rather than the element that means "a key", which is banned across every
               page here: it is monospace by default, and this app names two typefaces and no
               third. The box around each one is drawn in the stylesheet. *@
            <p class="palette-keys">
                <span><span class="key">&uarr;</span><span class="key">&darr;</span> to move</span>
                <span><span class="key">Enter</span> to go</span>
                <span><span class="key">Esc</span> to close</span>
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The most Powers to offer, and the most gear rows. The six steps are never truncated.
    ///
    /// <para>Counted per kind rather than over the whole list, so a word that matches eight Powers
    /// does not push every weapon off the bottom — and eight of each is still short enough that
    /// the book's rows are reachable by arrow key.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private const int PowerLimit = 8;

    private string _query = "";
    private int _active;
    private IReadOnlyList<Command> _found = [];
    private ElementReference _box;

    /// <summary>Whether the box still needs focus moving into it after this render.</summary>
    private bool _entering;

    /// <summary>The interop handle the key listener calls back through.</summary>
    private DotNetObjectReference<CommandPalette>? _self;

    private static string RowId(int index) => $"palette-row-{index}";

    /// <summary>
    /// What the box calls itself. <b>One sentence, decided in <see cref="Commands.Prompt"/></b>,
    /// because the banner's field is labelled with the same promise a second before this box is —
    /// and two spellings of it would be two promises made to one reader.
    ///
    /// <para><b>The <c>aria-label</c> is the label and the placeholder carries the same words as a
    /// hint.</b> A placeholder is not a label: it goes away the moment anybody types.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private string Prompt => Commands.Prompt;

    /// <summary>
    /// The heading this row wants above it, or null — set on the first row of a run of the same
    /// kind, and only for the kinds that are worth marking off.
    ///
    /// <para><b>The steps and the Powers carry none, deliberately.</b> They are the app's own
    /// controls and they were the whole of the palette for a long time; heading them would be
    /// furniture on the two things nobody needs told apart. The book's passages are the
    /// publisher's words rather than a control, and Chapter 6's rows lead somewhere else entirely
    /// — nothing on the page should leave the three looking like one voice.</para>
    ///
    /// <para><c>role="presentation"</c> and no id, so the listbox's children stay options alone,
    /// <c>aria-activedescendant</c> keeps naming a row and the indices stay the indices of
    /// <c>_found</c>. What it says is not load-bearing for a screen reader: every row under it
    /// carries its own detail line.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private string? GroupHeading(int index)
    {
        var kind = _found[index].Kind;

        if (index > 0 && _found[index - 1].Kind == kind) return null;

        return kind switch
        {
            CommandKind.Passage => "In the book",
            CommandKind.GearRow => "Gear from the book",
            _ => null
        };
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Which row the reader is on, for assistive technology. Null when there is nothing to be
    /// on — an empty result set has no current row, and naming one that is not rendered leaves
    /// a reference pointing at nothing, the same fault as an unconditional aria-controls on
    /// the budget breakdown.
    /// </summary>
    private string? ActiveId => _found.Count == 0 ? null : RowId(_active);

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        Commands.Changed += Refresh;
        Commands.Retyped += Replace;
        Commands.BookAnswered += Redraw;
        Recount();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The palette opened or closed somewhere else — the key, or a row being chosen.
    ///
    /// <para>The query is set on the way <i>in</i> rather than cleared on the way out, so that
    /// re-opening always starts from whatever opened it. Clearing on close would do the same thing
    /// until something closed the palette without coming through this component.</para>
    ///
    /// <para><b>And it is <see cref="Commands.TakeOpeningQuery"/> rather than a blank string,
    /// which is the whole of the banner field's mechanism on this side.</b> Somebody typing into
    /// the banner is typing into a control with no matcher of its own; the letter arrives here, in
    /// the box that has one, and everything below this line — the steps, the Powers, the pause,
    /// the book — runs exactly as it does for a letter typed into the box itself. Empty for every
    /// other way in.</para>
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <b>The whole body is dispatched, not just the redraw.</b> This runs on whatever thread
    /// raised the event, and one of the raisers is the key listener, which arrives from the
    /// browser rather than from Blazor — touching component state from there is the kind of
    /// race that shows up once in a hundred presses, and Blazor refuses it outright when it can
    /// tell. The reset above the redraw is component state too, so it goes inside as well.
    /// </remarks>
    private void Refresh() => _ = InvokeAsync(() =>
    {
        if (Commands.IsOpen)
        {
            _query = Commands.TakeOpeningQuery();
            _active = 0;
            _entering = true;

            // Asked for whatever the box now holds, which is empty for the chord and a word for
            // the banner's field. Empty, it clears whatever the book last answered — a question
            // nobody is asking — through the same path a keystroke takes, which is what makes an
            // answer already in flight land on nothing. Carrying a word, it is the first ask of
            // that word, and there is exactly one: the field does not search, so nothing has
            // asked before this.
            _ = Commands.AskTheBookAsync(_query);

            Recount();
        }

        StateHasChanged();
    });

    /// <summary>
    /// Somebody is still typing into the banner while the palette is already open, because focus
    /// has not crossed yet. Take the whole of what they have typed.
    ///
    /// <para><b>The same body as <see cref="Typed"/>, on purpose.</b> A letter that arrives here
    /// arrived in the banner instead of in this box only because an interop hop had not finished,
    /// and it must reach the same rows, the same pause and the same book it would have reached had
    /// it landed a few milliseconds later. Anything less is the palette answering a question
    /// nobody asked: it searched the first letter and stopped.</para>
    ///
    /// <para><b>A replacement, never an append.</b> The banner hands over the whole of its field on
    /// every input, so two of these arriving out of order — or one arriving twice — settle on the
    /// same text rather than on a doubled one.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private void Replace(string query) => _ = InvokeAsync(() =>
    {
        _query = query;
        _active = 0;

        // Asked before the rows are counted, for the reason `Typed` gives at length: the service
        // drops the book's rows before its first await, so counting afterwards is what puts this
        // render on the new query rather than on the last one's passages.
        _ = Commands.AskTheBookAsync(_query);

        Recount();
        StateHasChanged();
    });

    /// <summary>
    /// The book answered, or the offer of it moved. Redraw, and nothing else.
    ///
    /// <para><b>It is a second handler rather than a second raiser of <see cref="Commands.Changed"/>
    /// for one reason</b>: <see cref="Refresh"/> above empties the box whenever that event arrives
    /// while the palette is open, and the book's answer is caused by what is in the box. One event
    /// for both would clear the query a fifth of a second after it was typed.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private void Redraw() => _ = InvokeAsync(() =>
    {
        Recount();
        StateHasChanged();
    });

    /// <summary>
    /// The rows: the steps and the Powers the service matched, then the book's passages.
    ///
    /// <para><b>One flat list, so one index means one row.</b> <c>aria-activedescendant</c> names
    /// a row by an id derived from its position, and the arrow keys move through the same
    /// positions — a second list beside this one would be a second numbering, and the two would
    /// disagree the first time a group was empty. The book is appended rather than interleaved, so
    /// an answer arriving cannot move the row the reader has Enter poised over.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private void Recount()
    {
        _found = [.. Commands.Matching(_query, PowerLimit), .. Commands.Book];

        // The list can shrink under the current row — the book's answer replacing five rows with
        // none is the ordinary way — and a row named by aria-activedescendant that is no longer
        // rendered is the dangling reference this palette already has a test about.
        if (_active >= _found.Count) _active = 0;
    }

    private void Typed(ChangeEventArgs e)
    {
        _query = e.Value?.ToString() ?? "";

        // Back to the top on every keystroke. Keeping the position would leave the reader on
        // whatever row happened to be fourth in a list they have just replaced.
        _active = 0;

        // **Asked before the rows are counted, and that ordering is the whole of it.** The service
        // drops the book's rows the moment the query moves, and it does that in the part of this
        // call that runs before its first await — so counting afterwards is what puts the render
        // that follows this keystroke on the new query rather than showing the last question's
        // passages for the pause plus a round trip.
        //
        // Fire and forget past that point: the answer arrives through BookAnswered, and awaiting
        // it here would hold up the redraw of the rows that are already known.
        _ = Commands.AskTheBookAsync(_query);

        Recount();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The arrow keys, Enter and Escape.
    ///
    /// <para><b>Nothing here suppresses a default and nothing needs to.</b> Blazor decides
    /// that at render time rather than per event, so a blanket suppression on this element
    /// would stop the letters reaching the box and the palette could not be typed in. The
    /// keys handled here have no default worth suppressing on a single-line box: Enter
    /// submits nothing, because there is no form, and an arrow moves a caret on a line that
    /// does not wrap.</para>
    ///
    /// <para>Movement wraps, because a list this short is a ring rather than a page: pressing
    /// up from the first row means the last one.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private void Key(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        switch (e.Key)
        {
            case "Escape":
                Close();
                return;

            case "Enter":
                if (_active >= 0 && _active < _found.Count) Run(_found[_active]);
                return;

            case "ArrowDown":
                if (_found.Count > 0) _active = (_active + 1) % _found.Count;
                return;

            case "ArrowUp":
                if (_found.Count > 0) _active = (_active - 1 + _found.Count) % _found.Count;
                return;

            default:
                return;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Do the thing on the row. A step is a navigation; a Power is a request, because the
    /// editor that opens it is on another step and is the one place that knows how to price
    /// what the reader chooses there.
    /// </summary>
    private void Run(Command command)
    {
        switch (command.Kind)
        {
            case CommandKind.Power:
                Commands.RequestPower(command.Target);
                Nav.NavigateTo("build/characteristics");
                return;

            // A gear row is a request too, and it adds nothing to the character. It narrows the
            // Gear step's catalogue to the row and leaves the choosing where the choosing is —
            // the same relationship a Power has to its editor. Adding it here would be cheap
            // (mundane gear is free) and would still be the palette changing a character.
            case CommandKind.GearRow:
                Commands.RequestGearRow(command.Target);
                Nav.NavigateTo("build/gear");
                return;

            // A passage is a request too, and for the same reason: the page that can read one is
            // another page. What is handed over is the reader's own query, so /rules answers the
            // question they actually asked rather than a heading picked out of it — and the row
            // they chose is in that answer, a few rows down the same ranked list.
            case CommandKind.Passage:
                Commands.RequestSearch(command.Target);
                Nav.NavigateTo("rules");
                return;

            default:
                Commands.Close();
                Nav.NavigateTo(command.Target);
                return;
        }
    }

    private void Close() => Commands.Close();

    /// <summary>
    /// Focus follows the palette: into the box when it opens, back where it came from when it
    /// closes. Both are interop, because focus is not something a render tree expresses.
    /// </summary>
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (firstRender)
        {
            _self = DotNetObjectReference.Create(this);
            await Keys.Listen(_self);

            // Asked here as well as on every open, so the very first press of the chord draws a
            // box already labelled with what it will do. This component is created once by the
            // layout, long before anybody opens it.
            await Commands.NoteWhoIsAskingAsync();
        }

        if (Commands.IsOpen && _entering)
        {
            _entering = false;

            // **Who is here, and only then the caret.** Again on every open, because signing in
            // and out happens inside the visit and the answer decides both whether the book is
            // asked and whether the box says it will be — so it has to be settled *before* the box
            // can be typed into. The other order left a window one interop hop wide in which a
            // signed-out reader's first keystrokes were asked on behalf of an account that was
            // gone; `NoteWhoIsAskingAsync` drops whatever is already in flight, and the ask itself
            // re-reads the offer after its pause, so all three guards close the same window.
            await Commands.NoteWhoIsAskingAsync();
            await Keys.Enter(_box);
            return;
        }

        // Closed, and something may have been focused inside it. Putting focus back is what
        // stops this being a keyboard trap; the call does nothing when there is nothing
        // remembered, so an ordinary render costs one interop hop and no behaviour.
        if (!Commands.IsOpen) await Keys.Leave();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Called from the key listener. <c>InvokeAsync</c> because it arrives from outside
    /// Blazor's synchronisation context, and touching component state from there is the kind
    /// of race that shows up once in a hundred presses.
    /// </summary>
    [JSInvokable]
    public Task Toggle() => InvokeAsync(() =>
    {
        Commands.Toggle();
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    });

    public ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        Commands.Changed -= Refresh;
        Commands.Retyped -= Replace;
        Commands.BookAnswered -= Redraw;
        _self?.Dispose();
        return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
    }
}
